  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 bioRxiv preprint is still at an early stage of scholarly uptake, but it already shows measurable downstream use: one citing preprint, two documented reuse signals, and three scite mentions. The record points to interest from neurobiology and insect-vision researchers, especially those working on visual pathways and behavior in <i>Drosophila</i>.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article, <i>The organization of visual pathways in the Drosophila brain</i>, addresses a foundational question in visual neuroscience: how layered, retinotopic circuits in the fly optic lobes transform photoreceptor input into distinct pathways supporting behavior. The abstract frames the work as a system-wide account of how visually relevant computations are organized across parallel circuits, an approach that makes the preprint relevant not only to Drosophila neurobiology but also to broader questions of visual processing and perception.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Its impact record is modest but meaningful for a recent preprint. OpenAlex shows one citation, and the citing work is itself another bioRxiv preprint published in 2026, indicating immediate engagement within an active research stream rather than delayed uptake in a distant field. The citing landscape is concentrated in neuroscience and insect physiology, with institutional activity centered in Austria and Germany.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond citation, the work has two documented reuse signals and three scite mentions, suggesting that it has been noticed in the literature ecosystem even while formal citation counts remain low. The reuse record includes a biomedical article mention in Europe PMC and a preprint reuse signal, which together point to early-stage scholarly visibility rather than broad public attention.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The overall pattern is best read as an emerging contribution: strongly aligned with current questions in visual system organization, already taken up by at least one follow-on preprint, and present in downstream indexing and mention systems. With only a small citation base so far, the record supports a narrative of initial scholarly traction rather than established field-level influence.</p>",
